          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly avows the speaker's present love. In context, it answers Maria's concern that his apparent change may be temporary or imagined, and the oath-like construction emphatically presents the avowal as true.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt end for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"hun forstaar, jeg taler sandt\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal words avow love but do not themselves undertake a future action or obligation. The speaker's separate statement about living and dying with Maria may express a relational commitment, but that force should not be transferred onto the focal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil jeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"Although the reunion is highly emotional, the focal expression is a deliberate verbal answer to Maria's question and functions as an emphatic assertion, not as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt end for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The oath-like emphasis and reassurance are adequately captured as strengthened truth-presentation under T; no distinct residual core function is evident.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt end for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At an emotional reunion, Maria asks whether the speaker's apparent love is genuine and lasting rather than something he will regret tomorrow or has imagined from missing his lover. He answers with the focal avowal, embedded in an emphatic assurance.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no undertaking. In the immediately following sentence, the speaker separately presents Maria as the person with whom he intends to live for a long time and ultimately die. Maria cries because she understands that he speaks truly and is happy, showing uptake of the avowal; the evidence does not separately articulate her acceptance of precise commitment terms.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not confidently recognize the work from the supplied text alone.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the expression's avowing force and the represented sincerity of the speaker at that moment. It does not establish eventual fulfilment of the neighbouring future-oriented statement or define any formal relational obligation.\"}"
